.TH UFLIB 7 "September 2026" "uflib 4.0.0.8" "UFSRV Programmer's Manual"
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NAME
uflib \- foundational C library for the ufsrv messaging-server platform
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <uflib/utils.h>
.B #include <uflib/utils_crypto.h>
.B #include <uflib/adt/adt_hashtable.h>
.B #include <uflib/cdt/cdt_mpsc_queue.h>
.B #include <uflib/cdt/lockless_ringbuffer/cdt_lockless_ringbuffer.h>
.B #include <uflib/recycler_v2/recycler_v2.h>
.B #include <uflib/logger/logger.h>
.B #include <uflib/ufconfig/ufconfig.h>
.B #include <uflib/ufcommand/ufcommand.h>
.B #include <uflib/buffer_descriptor/buffer_descriptor.h>
.B #include <uflib/optional/ufoptional.h>
.fi
.sp
Link with
.BR \-luflib .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uflib
is the bottom layer of the ufsrv development stack.  It provides the
general-purpose software primitives every higher layer builds on: abstract
data types, lock-free concurrent data structures, an object recycler,
crypto and DB abstractions, networking helpers, an owning optional
container, a command registry, compression, JSON, protobuf, UUID generation,
a backend-agnostic logging interface, and utility routines.  Dependencies flow
strictly downward \(em uflib has no visibility of ufsrvcorelib, ufnetcorelib,
or any application server.
.PP
uflib builds
.B libuflib_static.a
(and, when
.BR BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON ,
.BR libuflib.so )
from a single OBJECT library.  It is consumed by the higher layers via
.BR "find_package(uflib REQUIRED)" " + " uflib::uflib .
The library is written in C17 with GNU extensions, requires Clang, and is
licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 or later.
.PP
This page describes version
.BR 4.0.0.8 " of "
.BR uflib ,
that being
.BR MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.INTERNAL
as defined in the installed
.BR version.h .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH MODULES
.\" ===================================================
.SS Abstract data types \&(adt) \(em \fBadt/\fP
Classical single-threaded containers.
.PP
.TP
.B hashtable_v2
Open-addressing hash table.
.TP
.B hopscotch_hashtable_v2
Hopscotch hash table (bounded probe sequences).
.TP
.B distinct_array
Array of unique elements.
.TP
.B linkedlist / doubly_linkedlist
Intrusive singly- and doubly-linked lists.
.TP
.B queue
FIFO queue.
.TP
.B minheap
Single-threaded binary min-heap (priority queue).  Opaque handle; pointer-key
and inline-int64 key modes; any-magnitude comparator; geometric growth.
.BR MinHeapDescribe (MinHeap, bd)
emits state as JSON (size, capacity, mode, load factor, entries).
.TP
.B locking_lru
Mutex-protected least-recently-used cache.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Concurrent data types \&(cdt) \(em \fBcdt/\fP
Lock-free and lock-based structures for multi-threaded hot paths, each
declared in its own header under
.IR cdt/ .
.PP
.TP
.B LamportQueue
Lock-free SPSC FIFO (two-index, one reserved slot).
.TP
.B LocklessMpscQueue
Intrusive lock-free MPSC (Vyukov).
.TP
.B LocklessFixedWidthHashMap
Lock-free fixed-width hash map (seq-lock + open addressing).
.TP
.B LocklessRingBuffer
Bounded copy-in/copy-out ring buffer with three concurrency modes \(em
SPSC, MPSC, and MPMC (Vyukov per-slot sequence numbers).
.TP
.B LocklessLru
Lock-free approximate least-recently-used cache (CLOCK second-chance
eviction, per-slot seqlocks, upsert
.BR LocklessLruSet / LocklessLruSetEx ).
.TP
.B LocklessMinHeap
Lock-free integer min-priority queue.  The concurrent companion to the
single-threaded
.BR MinHeap ,
and deliberately not a binary heap: a sift is a multi-word update on a
path and cannot be linearised portably, so this is a Harris lock-free
ordered list.  Inline
.B int64_t
keys with an opaque value, insert and delete-min only, no peek; reclamation
is quiescent
.RB ( LocklessMinHeapReclaim ).
.TP
.B ChaseLevStealingQueue
Chase-Lev work-stealing deque.
.TP
.B spinlock_anderson_t / SpinlockMcs
Anderson and MCS locks, with the spin-pause definitions they share in
.IR cdt_spinlock_defs.h .
.TP
.B LocklessTreiberStack
Shared ABA-protected intrusive LIFO free-list (used by the recycler and
hash map).
.\" ===================================================
.SS Recycler \&(recycler, recycler_v2)
Object pooling with optional persistence.  A recycler allocates objects in
slab-like groups, hands them out through a lock-free free stack, and \(em in
V2 \(em can CLOCK-evict idle objects to disk and re-materialise them on
demand.
.PP
.TP
.B RecyclerV2InitTypePool(config)
Register a type pool.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Get(handle, ctx, flags)
Lease an object (returns an
.BR InstanceHolderV2 ).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Put(ih, ctx, flags)
Return an object to the pool.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2Referenced(ih, n) / RecyclerV2UnReferenced(ih, n)
External reference counting (multi-reference).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2GetNewInstance(ih) / RecyclerV2DestroyInstance(ih)
Multi-holder aliases for sharing one object.
.TP
.B RecyclerV2GetCapacity(handle) / RecyclerV2GetLeasedCount(handle)
Capacity and occupancy queries.
.TP
.B DescribeRecycler(type_pool_name, provided)
Describe pool state as a JSON string (all pools, or one by exact name).
.TP
.B ListRecyclerTypes()
List every registered type name in one contiguous allocation (free once).
.TP
.B RecyclerV2DumpState(stream) / RecyclerV2DumpStateToBuffer()
Full debug/introspection snapshot as JSON.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Buffer descriptor \&(buffer_descriptor)
A growable string builder used for console/JSON output.
.PP
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorInit(bd, cap)
Allocate the backing buffer (clamped to [64, 1 MiB]).
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorAppendFormatted(bd, fmt, ...)
Append printf-style output, growing as needed.
.TP
.B BufferDescriptorRelease(bd)
Free the backing buffer.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Optional \&(optional)
An opaque container holding either one payload or nothing, following the
semantics of Java's
.B Optional
where they survive the crossing into C.  The payload is a
.BR "void *" ,
and every operation that can fail reports a
.B UfOptionalStatus
rather than throwing.
.sp
The container
.B owns
its payload.  A constructor takes ownership of a non-NULL payload on success,
.BR UfOptionalMap " and " UfOptionalFlatMap
take ownership of what the mapper publishes, and
.B UfOptionalTake
hands ownership back to the caller.  Release and transfer both go through the
destroy callback fixed when the payload was stored, so the container never has
to know what kind of payload it holds.  A NULL handle is
.B not
a container: every read accessor tolerates one and reports empty, so a host
that has not yet built an optional needs no guard branch at each use.
.sp
.TP
.B UfOptionalEmpty(destroy, context) / UfOptionalOf(value, destroy, context) / UfOptionalOfNullable(value, destroy, context)
Constructors.  The first two yield an empty container and reject a NULL
payload; the third collapses a NULL payload to empty.
.TP
.B UfOptionalClone(source, clone, destroy, context)
Deep-copy through a caller-supplied clone operation.
.TP
.B UfOptionalDestroy(optional)
Destroy a container, releasing the payload it still owns.
.TP
.B UfOptionalIsPresent(optional) / UfOptionalIsEmpty(optional) / UfOptionalGet(optional)
State and borrowed access.
.TP
.B UfOptionalTake(optional)
Take the payload, leaving the container empty.
.TP
.B UfOptionalOrElse(optional, other) / UfOptionalOrElseGet(optional, supplier, context)
The payload, or a fallback.  The supplier is consulted only when it is needed.
.TP
.B UfOptionalOrElseThrow(optional, out_value)
Java's
.BR orElseThrow ,
expressed as a status and an out-parameter.
.TP
.B UfOptionalIfPresent(optional, consumer, context) / UfOptionalFilter(optional, predicate, context)
Act on the payload; discard it unless it satisfies a predicate.
.TP
.B UfOptionalMap(optional, mapper, context, mapped_destroy, out_optional) / UfOptionalFlatMap(optional, mapper, context, out_optional)
Transform the payload into a new container, flat in the second case.  A mapper
that publishes a payload and
.B then
reports failure leaves the release to the library, so it must not release the
value itself.  The context given to the mapper is the one later handed to the
mapped payload's destroy callback.
.TP
.B UfOptionalSet(optional, value, destroy, context)
Replace the payload.  Re-setting the payload the container already holds is a
no-op on the payload itself.
.TP
.B UfOptionalLastStatus(optional)
The outcome of the most recent state-changing call.  Reads never record, and
neither do calls that found nothing to do:
.BR UfOptionalTake " and " UfOptionalFilter
record
.B UFOPTIONAL_OK
when they had a payload to work on and
.B UFOPTIONAL_EMPTY
when the container was already empty.
.sp
What the module does not do: it is not thread-safe.  A container is
single-owner, and if one is shared between threads synchronization belongs at
that boundary, outside this API.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Utilities \&(utils*.h)
String, file, crypto, time, thread, and encoding helpers:
.BR utils_str ,
.BR utils_file ,
.BR utils_crypto ,
.BR utils_secrets ,
.BR utils_time ,
.BR utils_threads ,
.BR utils_base64 ,
.BR utils_base64url ,
.BR base32 ,
.BR utils_hex ,
.BR utils_iec_quantity ,
.BR utils_urls ,
.BR utils_bits ,
.BR utils_collection_str ,
.BR utils_sys ,
and
.BR tokeniser .
.PP
.B utils_secrets
carries the AES-256-GCM envelope primitives used by
.BR ufconfig 's
encrypted fields: it produces and consumes
.B enc:v1:<nonce>:<ciphertext>:<tag>
values, reads a secrets file mapping a field's path to its key, and wipes key
material and plaintext.  It is usable on its own by anything that needs an
envelope rather than only by the configuration module.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Persistence and networking \&(db, net)
.B db/
is a MariaDB/MySQL abstraction;
.B net/
provides networking helpers.  Both resolve OpenSSL through
.BR find_package(OpenSSL) .
.\" ===================================================
.SS Ufsrv identifier \&(ufsrvuid)
The ufsrv-native user identifier: a timestamp, instance and sequence tuple
with reserved system-user values.  Generated, converted to and from binary and
encoded text, and compared.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvUidGenerate(uid) / UfsrvUidCopy(dst, src)
Create an identifier, or copy one.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidConvertToBinary(uid, buf) / UfsrvUidConvertSerialise(uid, buf)
Binary and serialised forms.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidCreateFromBinary(buf) / UfsrvUidCreateFromEncodedText(text)
Reconstruct an identifier from either form.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidGetTimestamp(uid) / UfsrvUidGetInstanceId(uid) / UfsrvUidGetSequenceId(uid)
The components the identifier is built from.
.TP
.B UfsrvUidIsSystemUser(uid) / UfsrvUidRawDataSystemUser()
The reserved system-user range.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Scheduled jobs \&(scheduled_jobs)
Scheduled task execution.
.\" ===================================================
.SS File loader \&(file_loader, thread_worker)
Caching a file's contents and reloading it when it changes, and a service that
loads files concurrently across registered threads.
.PP
.TP
.B FileLoaderLoadIfModified(loader, path) / FileLoaderReset(loader)
Load on change; drop the cached copy.
.TP
.B FileLoaderCompareHashes(a, b)
Detect change by content digest rather than by timestamp.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceInit(svc, ...) / FileLoaderServiceDestroy(svc)
Start and stop the concurrent loading service.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceRegisterThread(svc, ...) / FileLoaderServiceGetThread(svc)
Attach a worker; retrieve its handle.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceAddFile(svc, path) / FileLoaderServiceGetFileContent(svc, ...)
Submit a file and read back its contents.
.TP
.B FileLoaderServiceGetFileInfo(svc, ...) / FileLoaderServiceCapacity(svc) / FileLoaderServiceHeartbeat(svc)
Per-file state, and the service's own health.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Logging \&(logger) \(em \fBlogger/\fP
Records are emitted at eight ordered severity ranks through an opaque
.B UfLogger
handle, to syslog, stderr, stdout, or a size-bounded file.
.PP
The interface names no backend.  A caller states a category, a severity
floor, a destination and an output pattern; the library binds the
implementation and never reports which one it chose.  Records carry the
caller's own source location, and per-thread context can be rendered into
the pattern so that a request identifier set once appears on every record
that request produces.
.PP
Operations a build cannot honour are refused with
.B UF_LOGGER_STATUS_ERR_UNSUPPORTED
rather than silently ignored.
.B UfLoggerGetCapabilities
reports in advance what the bound implementation supports, and
.B UfLoggerDescribeConfiguration
renders the effective configuration as JSON.
.PP
The backend is folded into
.BR libuflib :
a consumer of
.B uflib::uflib
needs no additional include path, no additional link flag, and no knowledge
that a third-party logging library is involved.
.\" ===================================================
.SS QR code \&(qr_code)
Reading and rendering, each behind its own capability so neither drags in the
other's dependency.  Reading uses the vendored
.B quirc
decoder and is built with
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_QUIRC ;
rendering uses libqrencode and is built with
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_QRENCODE .
.PP
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeDecode(image, width, height, stride, text, cap, len)
Read the QR symbol in a greyscale image.
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeCameraGenerate(device, width, height, timeout_ms, text, cap, len)
Open a V4L2 capture device, read a frame from it, and return the decoded text.
.TP
.B UfUtilsQrCodeEncodeText(text, out_matrix)
Encode text as a QR symbol, returned as a module matrix rather than an image.
.\" ===================================================
.SS ufconfig
.B ufconfig
is a configuration loader for ufsrv-class servers.  It parses a document
written in a Lua-styled table syntax, validates it against a schema,
canonicalises it, and serves the result through an opaque handle.
.sp
The syntax mimics Lua and is
.B not
evaluated: there is no expression language, no standard library, and an
operator in a document is a parse error rather than something computed.
.sp
.B The library holds no schema.
A schema describes one application's configuration \- which fields exist, what
they default to, what they must satisfy \- and that is the application's
business.  It is generated from the application's own files by a separate
pipeline, compiled into the application, and injected at
.BR UfConfigCreate .
.sp
.nf
UfConfigDescriptor d = {
    .fields      = g_ufconfig_fields,     \"* generated from the schema *\"
    .field_count = (size_t)g_ufconfig_field_count,
    .lookup      = UfConfigLookupPath,    \"* generated index *\"
    .uf_logger   = app_logger,            \"* optional; NULL logs nothing *\"
    .kind        = UF_CONFIG_BACKEND_FILE,
};

UfConfig *h = NULL;
UfConfigCreate(&h, &d);

UfConfigLoadOptions opt = { .size = sizeof(opt), .version = 1,
                            .mode = UF_CONFIG_LOAD_STRICT };
UfConfigLoadFile(h, "etc/ufsrvwebsock.config.lua", &opt, NULL);

UfConfigValue v;
UfConfigGetField(h, "ufsrv.main_listener_port", &v);
UfConfigDestroy(h);
.fi
.sp
.B Loading from a store
rather than a file is the same call with a different descriptor: the origin is
stated once, at create, and the API does not change.
.B UF_CONFIG_BACKEND_REDIS
and
.B UF_CONFIG_BACKEND_SQL
name it, with the endpoint beside it:
.sp
.nf
\&.kind   = UF_CONFIG_BACKEND_REDIS,
\&.redis  = { .address = "10.0.0.5", .port = 6379,
            .namespace = "alice", .config_name = "ufsrvwebsock" },
.fi
.sp
and for SQL the address carries the connection string rather than a host and
port pair:
.sp
.nf
\&.kind   = UF_CONFIG_BACKEND_SQL,
\&.sql    = { .address = "host=10.0.0.6;port=3306;user=ufsrv;password=...;database=ufsrv",
            .ns = "alice", .config_name = "ufsrvwebsock" },
.fi
.sp
followed by
.BR UfConfigLoad ,
.BR UfConfigPersist " and " UfConfigReload .
The Redis driver is built only when the library is configured with
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_HIREDIS ,
and hiredis is then the consumer's to link \- the library does not fold it
into the archive.
.B UFLIB_CAPABILITY_SQL
is the same arrangement for the SQL driver, and goes further: libmariadb is not
vendored at all, so it is looked for only when that option is set, and the
client remains the consumer's to link.
.sp
.B Values are borrowed.
Strings and byte runs in
.B UfConfigValue
stay valid while the handle lives, and across a reload for any path whose value
did not change.  A caller that needs them to outlive the handle must copy them.
.B Namespace handles
are the opposite: every one a walk is given is the caller's to
.BR free .
.sp
.B Reload is synchronous.
Whether it is called from a timer, a thread or a request path is the caller's
decision; the module does not schedule anything.  A reload reports drift per
field and, on failure, leaves the last good configuration in force.
.sp
.B Encrypted values.
A field whose schema declares
.B encrypted = true
holds an envelope rather than a secret in the clear, and a caller reading it
receives the plaintext.  The envelope stays in the document, in every
serialised form, in the digest, and in anything written back to a store, so a
configuration containing secrets remains safe to copy between hosts and to keep
in version control.
.sp
.nf
db_password = { type = \"string\", required = false, encrypted = true }
.fi
.sp
The value is
.B enc:v1:<nonce_hex>:<ciphertext_hex>:<tag_hex>
\- AES-256-GCM, a 12-byte nonce drawn fresh per encryption, and a 16-byte
authentication tag.  A wrong key and a tampered ciphertext are the same
failure, because the tag does not verify, and the two are deliberately
indistinguishable.
.sp
Keys are per field, held in a secrets file of
.B path=key
lines, one 64-hex-character key per full node path:
.sp
.nf
# /opt/ufsrv/etc/secrets/ufsrv.secrets
ufsrv.ufnet.db_backend.password=3f2a...e9
.fi
.sp
The file is named by
.B UfConfigDescriptor.config_file_secrets
at create.  When the descriptor names none,
.B ufconfig.secrets
in the process's working directory is used instead; when neither can be read
the handle is refused.  A path that
.B is
named but cannot be read is an error and does not fall back, because honouring
a default after an explicit path failed would let anyone able to create a file
in the working directory substitute the keys.
.sp
The mechanism is driven by the
.B schema
declaring an encrypted field, not by any document containing one \- not every
field is mandatory, and an encrypted field may be absent from a given document
altogether.  A schema with no such field never opens a file and creates handles
exactly as before.  Where one is declared, every field's key is resolved at
create, so a server that cannot read its keys does not start and the load path
stays free of filesystem access.
.sp
.B It fails closed.
A value on an encrypted field that carries no
.B enc:v1:
prefix refuses the load.  Were the prefix optional, deleting four characters
from a configuration file would convert a secret into an accepted plaintext and
the declaration would enforce nothing.  An encrypted field is also
.B read-only
at runtime: the generator refuses
.B encrypted
together with
.BR mutable ,
and refuses it alongside
.B default
(a default is plaintext, and the decrypt step runs over a materialised default
exactly as over a document value) and on any non-string type.
.sp
Decryption happens before any declared transforms and before validation, so a
length, a
.B one_of
or a format on the field constrains the secret and not its envelope.  A
rejected secret is never quoted back:
.B UfConfigLastError
names the rule that refused it and masks the value.
.sp
What the module does not do: it does not manage or rotate keys, and it does not
wipe the arena, so a decrypted plaintext remains in arena memory until the
handle is destroyed.  Envelopes are produced by
.BR UflibSecretEncrypt .
\" ===================================================
.SS Command registry \&(ufcommand)
A registry of named commands with alias expansion and keyboard bindings, and
the parser that dispatches against it.  This is what a console, a dispatcher
or a REPL is built on.
.sp
Commands and aliases share
.B one namespace
and are canonicalised on the way in, so one command has one spelling.
Registering either over the other's name is refused rather than resolved by
precedence.  A command name may be of any depth, bounded only by
.BR max_command_components ,
and at any depth the longest registered name wins, so
.B remote
and
.B remote add
coexist.  Aliases expand with positional substitution, chained, with cycle
detection and a depth bound
.RB ( max_alias_depth ,
32 by default).
.sp
The parser holds no registry: each execution names the one to dispatch
against.
.sp
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryCreate(registry, config) / UfCommandRegistryDestroy(registry)
Create and destroy a registry.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryAdd(registry, definition) / UfCommandRegistryRemove(registry, name)
Register and unregister a command.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryAddAlias(registry, alias) / UfCommandRegistryRemoveAlias(registry, alias)
Alias registration and removal.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryAddBinding(registry, binding) / UfCommandRegistryRemoveBinding(registry, modifiers, ...)
Keystroke-binding registration and removal.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryFind(registry, name, ...)
Resolve a name without dispatching.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryGetKeyConfig(registry, ...) / UfCommandRegistrySetKeyConfig(registry, config)
The modifier set a binding is dispatched under.
.TP
.B UfCommandParserCreate(config, out_parser) / UfCommandParserDestroy(parser)
Create and destroy a dispatcher.
.TP
.B UfCommandParserExecute(parser, registry, ...) / UfCommandParserExecuteBinding(parser, registry, ...) / UfCommandParserExecuteDefaultBinding(parser, registry, ...)
Dispatch a line, a specific keystroke sequence, or the default binding.
.TP
.B UfCommandResultGetResolvedCommand(result) / UfCommandResultGetArgs(result) / UfCommandResultGetArgCount(result)
What the line resolved to.
.TP
.B UfCommandResultGetStatus(result) / UfCommandResultGetDispatchStatus(result) / UfCommandResultGetHandlerStatus(result)
The dispatch outcome and the handler's own outcome, kept apart.
.TP
.B UfCommandStatusName(status)
Render a status as text.
.TP
.B UfCommandContextCreate(config, out_context) / UfCommandContextDestroy(ctx) / UfCommandContextSetUserData(ctx, ...) / UfCommandContextGetUserData(ctx)
Caller state carried through a dispatch.
.TP
.B UfCommandRegistryLoadUserConfig(registry, path) / UfCommandRegistrySaveUserConfig(registry, path)
Load and save a user configuration of aliases and bindings.
.sp
.B Predictive completion
is an engine the registry owns and creates only when the configuration asks
for it with
.BR completion_enabled ,
reached through
.BR UfCommandCompletionGetCompletion .
A caller then asks through
.BR UfCommandCompletionSuggest ,
walks the candidates with
.BR UfCommandCompletionResultGetCandidate " and " UfCommandCompletionResultGetCount ,
and releases the result with
.BR UfCommandCompletionResultDestroy .
Candidate sources are registered separately through
.BR UfCommandCompletionAddProvider ,
which is the seam a host uses to supply its own; command history is one such
provider.  Off by default, so a host that never asks for a suggestion carries
neither the engine nor its per-call lookup.
.sp
.B Telemetry
is likewise opt-in
.RB ( telemetry_enabled ),
and is an observer rather than a log: a caller installs one with
.BR UfCommandRegistrySetTelemetry ,
reads it back through
.BR UfCommandRegistryGetTelemetry ,
and writes it as JSON with
.BR UfCommandTelemetrySaveJson .
.sp
The logger in the registry configuration is
.B borrowed
and must outlive every registry that references it, so create it first and
destroy it last.
\" ===================================================
.SH THIRD-PARTY PACKAGES
Seven third-party libraries are carried verbatim under
.IR packages/ ,
each built as a private static archive and folded into
.BR libuflib ,
so a consumer links nothing extra for them.  The one exception is
.BR hiredis ,
which is never folded in.
.PP
.TP
.B K12
KangarooTwelve, the Keccak Team's extendable-output hash, taken from XKCP
under a public-domain dedication.  Only one Keccak-p[1600] implementation may
be compiled at a time, because every variant defines the same permute symbols;
the Optimized64 variant is the one in use.
.TP
.B lzf
Marc Lehmann's LZF \(em fast, lossless, byte-oriented compression, trading
ratio for speed.  BSD 2-clause.
.TP
.B mjson
Cesanta's JSON parser.  MIT.
.TP
.B protobuf-c
Protocol Buffers in C, by Dave Benson and the protobuf-c authors.  BSD
2-clause.
.TP
.B quirc
Daniel Beer's QR-code recognition library.  ISC.  Folded in only under
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_QUIRC ,
and called into only by the QR-code module.
.TP
.B zlog
Hardy Simpson's thread-safe C logging library, vendored at 1.2.18.  Apache
License 2.0.  It is the backend behind the
.B UfLogger
interface, which names no implementation.
.TP
.B hiredis
The minimal C client for Redis, vendored at 0.14.0.  BSD 3-clause.  It is
confined to the configuration module's Redis driver and to nothing else in the
library.  That driver is built only under
.BR UFLIB_CAPABILITY_HIREDIS ;
hiredis itself is never built or folded in here, and is the consumer's to link.
Its asynchronous and TLS transports are not carried.
.PP
Two further third-party originals \(em a UUID v4 generator and Amazon's
backoff algorithm \(em are compiled into the library rather than carried here.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH CONVENTIONS
Public functions are
.B PascalCase
with a module prefix, are annotated
.BR PUBLIC_API ,
and are declared in installed headers.
Opaque handles hide implementation layout; private headers are never
installed.  Lock-free CDTs are additionally designed against the correctness
criteria of ABA, memory ordering, false sharing, reclamation and testing.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
/* Lock-free SPSC ring buffer */
LocklessRingBuffer *ring =
    LocklessRingBufferCreate(1024, sizeof(int), LOCKLESS_RINGBUF_MODE_SPSC);

int in = 42, out;
while (!LocklessRingBufferTryPush(ring, &in)) { /* full: back off */ }
while (!LocklessRingBufferTryPop(ring, &out))  { /* empty: poll  */ }

LocklessRingBufferDestroy(ring);
.fi
